<p>A purchase is one business change even when its data has several owners. The stock reductions, order 103 and its two lines must all commit, or all be discarded. Once these facts cross database partitions, a successful local commit is only one part of that promise.</p>

<p>Replication and transaction commit answer different questions. Replicas of one partition need to agree about that partition’s history. A transaction spanning several partitions needs one outcome for all of its participants. A majority of the replicas holding the stock cannot decide that an unrelated order partition has committed its part.</p>

<p>We rewind the shop to just before order 103. P7 has stock 1 and P3 has stock 12; Mina wants the mug and two packs of tea. For this article’s placement example, participant A owns product stock and participant B owns the order and lines. This deliberately crosses the boundary that tenant co-location can keep local. The protocol drawings are conceptual; a separate PostgreSQL experiment demonstrates prepared-state recovery on one machine.</p>

<h2 id="two-local-commits-leave-a-gap">Two local commits leave a gap</h2>

<p>Suppose the application commits the stock changes on A, then creates order 103 on B. If B rejects an order line, A has already committed. Rolling back B cannot return the stock on A. Reversing the commits changes the gap: the order can survive without its stock reservation.</p>

<p>The disposable experiment makes that failure visible. It reduces P7 from 1 to 0 and P3 from 12 to 10, then commits those changes. On the second database it tries to insert an order line with quantity 0. The line’s check constraint rejects it and that transaction rolls back. The result is no order 103 and stock already taken. Both databases have behaved correctly for their local transactions.</p>

<p>A compensating change can return stock later, but it is another transaction. Other work can observe or act on the intermediate state, and the compensation can itself fail. That may be a valid business process if its states and recovery are designed. It is a different contract from accepting the purchase atomically.</p>

<h2 id="which-agreement-is-being-made">Which agreement is being made?</h2>

